.TH ELFCHECK 1 "2026-11-01" "elfcheck v1.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
elfcheck \- validate and inspect ELF64 binaries
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B elfcheck
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIfile\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B elfcheck
reads an ELF64 little-endian file, validates its structure, and prints a
report covering the ELF header, program and section headers, dynamic
dependencies, GNU notes, and hardening posture (checksec-style). It can
also disassemble executable sections, dump section contents, and emit the
whole analysis as JSON.
.PP
It is a freestanding static binary: no libc, direct Linux x86-64 syscalls
only. Only ELF64 little-endian inputs are accepted; ELF32 and big-endian
files are rejected explicitly.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Output modes
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit the analysis as a single-line JSON object instead of the text report.
.TP
.B \-\-disasm\fR, \fB\-d
Disassemble the executable .text section (Intel syntax, with addresses and
raw opcodes). Direct branch targets are annotated with the matching function
symbol; in relocatable .o files, PC-relative relocations are resolved.
.TP
.B \-\-function \fIname\fR
Disassemble only the named function (requires a sized symbol from
\&.symtab or .dynsym).
.TP
.B \-\-start\-address \fIhex\fR
Start disassembly at a hexadecimal address inside the executable section.
.TP
.B \-\-count \fIn\fR
Limit disassembly to n instructions.
.TP
.B \-\-triage\fR, \fB\-t
Single pass flagging anomalies by severity (HIGH/MED/LOW/INFO) with a
summary line. Exits 2 when any HIGH or MEDIUM finding is present.
.TP
.B \-\-help\fR, \fB\-h
Print usage and exit 0.
.TP
.B \-\-version\fR, \fB\-v
Print version and exit 0.
.SS Section filters
By default the report prints its standard sections; pass filters to show
only what you need. Symbol and relocation tables are opt-in because they
can be large.
.TP
.B \-\-header\fR, \fB\-\-segments\fR, \fB\-\-sections
ELF header, program headers, section headers.
.TP
.B \-\-validate
Structural validation findings.
.TP
.B \-\-security
Advisory red flags: W^X segments/sections, executable stack, entry point
outside executable segments, stripped section headers.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic
Dynamic linking: needed libraries, imported symbols with GNU versions, and
the full .dynamic tag table (readelf \-d equivalent).
.TP
.B \-\-hardening
RELRO, stack canary, NX, PIE, CET (IBT/SHSTK), RPATH/RUNPATH, fortified
functions.
.TP
.B \-\-notes
GNU build-id, ABI tag and x86 feature properties.
.TP
.B \-\-mapping
Section to segment mapping (readelf \-l equivalent).
.TP
.B \-\-symbols\fR, \fB\-\-relocs
Symbol tables (with GNU symbol versions) and REL/RELA relocations.
.TP
.B \-\-entropy
Per-section Shannon entropy (bits/byte, 0\(en8). A high-entropy executable
section is tagged [high] and is a classic sign of packed, encrypted or
compressed code; it is also raised as a MED triage finding.
.SS Section dumps
.TP
.B \-\-hex\-dump\fR, \fB\-x \fIname\fR
Hex dump of a named section, byte-compatible with
.BR "readelf \-x" .
.TP
.B \-\-string\-dump\fR, \fB\-p \fIname\fR
Printable strings of a named section, like
.BR "readelf \-p" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Valid ELF, no structural issues and no HIGH/MED triage finding.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error, unreadable file, or not an ELF file.
.TP
.B 2
Structural inconsistencies found, or a HIGH/MED triage finding tripped the
gate.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Inspect a binary:
.B elfcheck /bin/ls
.TP
Quick security verdict usable as a CI gate:
.BR "elfcheck \-\-triage app && ./app" ""
.TP
JSON for scripting:
.BR "elfcheck \-\-json /bin/ls | jq .hardening" ""
.TP
Disassemble one function:
.BR "elfcheck \-\-function main ./app" ""
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR readelf (1),
.BR objdump (1),
.BR file (1)
